\h Daniel
\toc1 Daniel
\toc2 Daniel
\toc3 Dan.
\mt1 Daniel
\mt3 (or Daniyyel)
\is1 Introduction
\ip This document is about \bk Daniel\bk* (also spelt later as ‘Daniyyel’). He was captured as a young man in Yehudah (Judah) and taken to serve in Babylon. He wrote this at a time when the Jewish people were suffering a lot of mocking and persecution by those who worshipped false gods. Daniel became on of the highest officials in Babylon and we learn here about his obedience in serving God during the reigns of King Nevukadnetstsar (Nebuchadnezzar), Belshatstsar (Belshazzar), Dareyavesh (Darius), and Koresh (Cyrus). This document also has some brief but important sections telling about his three friends Hananyah, Meyshak, and Azaryah (perhaps better known as Shadrak, Meyshak, and Avednego).
\ip God's revelations which Daniel prophesied are also included here, concerning the strengthening and destruction of other nations starting with Babylon. It's also prophesied here about what would happen in the ‘end-times’.
\ip One of the themes of this document is about how God determines everything that happens in this world. He's the one who forms nations, and he's the one who determines their end. He also looks after his people.
\ip This document about Daniel was written with parts in Hebrew (1:1–2:3; 7:1–12:13) and parts in Aramaic (2:4–7:28). (Modern Syria is in the former Aram region. Aramaic is most closely related to Hebrew, Syriac, and Phoenician, and was written in a script derived from the Phoenician alphabet.)
\iot Main components of this account
\io1 God's help for Daniel and his friends \ior 1:1–6:28\ior*
\io1 God's revelations to Daniel through visions \ior 7:1–12:13\ior*
\io2 a. The four creatures \ior 7:1-28\ior*
\io2 b. The ram and the goat \ior 8:1–9:27\ior*
\io2 c. The heavenly messenger \ior 10:1–11:45\ior*
\io2 d. The time of the end \ior 12:1-13\ior*
\rem This is still an early look into the unpolished text of the Open English Translation of the Bible. Please double-check the text in advance before using in public.
\ie
\c 1
\ms1 The account about Daniel and his friends
\mr (1:1–6:28)
\s1 Daniel and his friends in Babylon
\rem /s1 Daniel in Nebuchadnezzar's Court; The Young Men at Nebuchadnezzar's Court; Four Young Israelites at the Babylonian Court; Daniel's Training in Babylon; Daniel and his companions
\p
\v 1 In the third year¦356354 of¦356357 Yehudah's¦356360 King Yehoyakim's¦356357 reign¦356356, Babylon's¦356365 King Nevukadnetstsar \add !(Nebuchadnezzar¦356362)\add* came¦356361 to Yerushalem¦356366 \add !(Jerusalem¦356366)\add* and¦356367 besieged \add @the city\add*.\x + \xo 1:1: \xt 2Ki 24:1; 2Ch 36:5-7.\x*
\v 2 \add After two years,\add* the master¦356371 \add ≈allowed King¦356376 Yehoyakim¦356375 to be defeated by\add* Nevukadnetstsar who then¦356370 took some of the items out of the temple and¦356370 took them to Shinar¦356387 \add !(Babylonia¦356387)\add* where he placed them in his god's temple storerooms.\x + \xo 1:2-4: \xt 2Ki 20:17-18; 24:10-16; 2Ch 36:10; Isa 39:7-8.\x*
\p
\v 3 \add Some time later,\add* King¦356399 \add Nevukadnetstsar\add* commanded his chief¦356401 official¦356402 Ashpenaz¦356400 to bring¦356403 him some of the young Israeli¦356405 men, from both their royal¦356407 family and¦356398 from \add ≈some of the prominent families\add*.
\v 4 \add They had to be\add* good-looking young men without obvious defects, wise and well-educated, who would be competent for \add future\add* \add ≈work\add* in the palace¦356435. They would be taught¦356437 the language¦356439 and literature of¦356440 the Chaldeans¦356440,
\v 5 and king assigned¦356442 a daily portion of¦356445 food and wine¦356453 for them \add ≈from his own table\add*. They¦356459 would be trained for three¦356457 years¦356456 \add before\add* \add ≈entering the¦356461 king's¦356461 service\add*.
\v 6 Among¦356464 the \add ≈young men\add* from Yehudah¦356466 \add who were chosen\add* were Daniel¦356467, Hananyah¦356468, Misha'el¦356469, and¦356470 Azaryah¦356470,
\v 7 \add ≈but¦356472\add* \add @Ashpenaz\add* \add ≈named\add* them¦356473 Belteshatstsar, Shadrak¦356481, Meyshak¦356483, and¦356472 Avednego \add respectively\add*.
\p
\v 8 \add ≈However\add* Daniel¦356489 \add ≈decided\add* that¦356493 he wouldn't eat the¦356498 king's¦356498 fancy food¦356497 or drink¦356500 his¦356500 wine¦356499 \add ≈because it¦356488 wasn't all ‘kosher’\add*, so he requested permission from¦356502 \add @Ashpenaz\add* to \add ≈eat an alternative diet\add*.
\v 9 \add ≈Now\add* God \add ≈had caused\add* the¦356509 chief¦356516 official¦356517 to \add ≈like and¦356514 respect\add* Daniel¦356512,
\v 10 \add ≈but¦356519\add* he \add ≈queried\add*, “I'm afraid¦356523 of my master¦356527 the king, who's¦356529 assigned¦356530 your food¦356533 and¦356519 drink¦356536—\add ≈if he saw¦356539 you guys\add* looking¦356543 worse than¦356544 the \add ≈others of\add* your own age¦356548 then¦356519 \add ≈I'd risk losing my head¦356552 if the king got angry\add*.”
\p
\v 11 \add ≈So¦356555\add* Daniel¦356556 asked the steward that \add @Ashpenaz\add* had¦356561 assigned¦356561 over \add @the four of¦356562 them\add*,
\v 12 “Please test¦356571 your¦356576 servants¦356576 for¦356581 ten¦356578 days¦356577: let us¦356581 be given¦356579 some¦356582 vegetables¦356584 to eat¦356585 and water¦356586 to drink¦356587,
\v 13 then¦356589 \add after that\add*, \add ≈see¦356599 how we look compared to¦356590 the other young men\add* who eat¦356594 the king's¦356597 choice food. Then¦356589 \add ≈you¦356599 can make the best decision from the evidence\add*.”
\p
\v 14 \add ≈So¦356605\add* the steward \add ≈agreed to that¦356608\add* and¦356609 \add started\add* \add ≈the ten-day trial\add*.
\v 15 At the end of the ten¦356615 days¦356614, they looked better and¦356619 healthier than all the young men who'd been eating¦356626 the king's¦356629 fancy food,
\v 16 \add ≈so\add* \add after that,\add* the steward \add just\add* gave¦356639 them¦356640 vegetables¦356641 to¦356640 eat instead of the choice food¦356636 and¦356631 wine¦356637.
\p
\v 17 \add ≈So¦356643\add* God¦356648 gave¦356646 those¦356644 four¦356645 young men knowledge¦356649 and insight into all¦356651 literature, and wisdom¦356654, and Daniel¦356655 \add ≈was able to interpret\add* any dreams¦356660 and visions¦356659.
\p
\v 18 At the end¦356662 of \add @the three years\add*\x + \xo 1:18 \xt 1:5\x* when the king¦356667 ordered them to be brought in, the chief¦356670 official¦356671 \add Ashpenaz\add* brought them in to King¦356667 Nevukadnetstsar¦356673.
\v 19 The¦356677 king talked with \add each of\add* them¦356680 and \add realised that\add* \add ≈none of the others were as capable as\add* Daniel¦356681, Hananyah¦356682, Misha'el¦356683, and Azaryah¦356684, \add ≈so they¦356685 ended up in¦356686 the king's¦356687 service\add*—
\v 20 in every¦356689 matter¦356690 of wisdom¦356691 and understanding¦356692 which¦356693 the king¦356697 asked them about¦356690, he¦356698 found¦356698 them ten¦356699 times better than all¦356702 the magicians¦356704 and astrologers \add ≈from throughout\add* his¦356709 entire¦356702 kingdom¦356709.
\v 21 Daniel¦356712 continued \add serving\add* there through to the first year¦356715 of the reign of King¦356718 Koresh¦356717 \add !(Cyrus¦356717)\add*.
\c 2
\s1 Nevukadnetstsar's dream
\rem /s1 Daniel's wisdom; Nebuchadnezzar's Dream
\p
\v 1 \add Back in\add* the second year¦356721 of King Nevukadnetstsar's¦356724 reign¦356723, \add *the king\add* had had some dreams¦356725 that troubled him—leaving him unable to sleep¦356730.
\v 2 \add *He¦356740\add* summoned the magicians¦356737, fortune-tellers, sorcerers¦356739, and \add ≈astrologers\add* to \add ≈explain\add* the dreams¦356743 to \add *him\add*, so they¦356744 came¦356744 in and stood¦356745 in front¦356746 of¦356746 the king¦356747.
\v 3 “I've¦356753 had¦356753 a dream¦356753,” the king¦356751 told them, “and¦356754 my¦356755 spirit¦356755 is \add ≈anxious to understand\add* \add *it\add*.”
\v 4 The¦356762 \add ≈astrologers\add* spoke¦356761 to¦356763 the king in Aramaic¦356764, “Long live¦356767 King¦356763 Nevukadnetstsar. Tell¦356768 us what you dreamt¦356769, and¦356761 then¦356761 we'll¦356773 give you the interpretation¦356772.”
\p
\v 5 “\add ≈I've already made my decision\add*,” the king¦356776 replied. “If¦356783 you all can't¦356784 tell me¦356785 the dream¦356786 and \add then give\add* its¦356787 interpretation¦356787, you'll all be¦356789 torn limb from limb and your¦356790 houses¦356790 made into a rubbish heap.
\v 6 But¦356794 if¦356794 you all explain the dream and its interpretation, you'll receive¦356802 gifts¦356798 from me, and a reward¦356799 and great¦356801 honour¦356800. So¦356794 tell me the dream and its interpretation.”
\p
\v 7 “Let¦356814 the king¦356814 tell¦356816 his servants¦356817 the dream¦356815,” they¦356813 \add ≈insisted\add*, “and¦356818 we'll¦356819 give the interpretation¦356818.”
\p
\v 8 “I know¦356827 for certain¦356826 that¦356829 you're all trying to¦356833 gain time¦356830,” the¦356841 king¦356822 answered. “\add ≈However, I've made up my mind\add*
\v 9 that if¦356844 you all don't¦356847 \add ≈tell me what my dream was\add*, \add ≈then your¦356852 fate is already clear\add* because you've \add obviously\add* agreed¦356856 together¦356856 to¦356848 \add ≈keep feeding me with lies¦356854 and made-up stories until¦356861 I give in\add*. So then, tell me what \add &my\add* dream was, and then I'll¦356869 \add ≈be confident\add* that you can give me its¦356871 interpretation¦356871.”
\p
\v 10 “There's¦356883 no one \add ≈in the whole world who¦356888 could tell\add* the king that,” the \add ≈astrologers\add* answered¦356880 the king. “No great¦356900 and¦356901 powerful¦356901 king has¦356905 asked¦356905 any¦356906 magician¦356908 or enchanter or \add ≈astrologer¦356909\add* to do that \add before\add*!
\v 11 What¦356924 you're requesting, \add ≈your majesty\add*, is difficult¦356917, and¦356918 \add ≈no human can tell you\add* \add what you dreamt\add*—only¦356925 the gods¦356926.”
\v 12 \add ≈That angered the king¦356939, and¦356941 he got so furious¦356941 that¦356938 he ordered for all¦356945 the Babylonian wise¦356946 men¦356946 to be executed\add*.
\p
\v 13 \add ≈When¦356949\add* the decree¦356949 went¦356950 out¦356950 that \add all\add* the wise¦356951 men¦356951 were to \add be rounded up to\add* be executed¦356956, Daniel¦356954 and¦356949 his friends¦356955 \add were included\add*.
\rem /s1 God Shows Daniel What the Dream Means
\v 14 Aryok¦356964 was the captain of the king's¦356969 guards who'd been sent¦356961 to execute¦356967 the Babylonian wise men, \add but\add* Daniel¦356960 spoke to him with \add ≈wise and¦356963 prudent\add* \add caution\add*.
\p
\v 15 He¦356976 asked the king's¦356982 commander Aryok, “Why \add ≈did the king come to that sudden decision\add*?” Aryok \add ≈explained what had happened\add*,
\v 16 \add ≈so¦356998\add* Daniel¦357003 went¦356999 to the king¦357003 and¦356998 requested some time¦357005 so he¦357006 would be able to give¦357006 the interpretation¦357009 to \add *him\add*.
\p
\rem /s1 God Reveals Nebuchadnezzar's Dream
\v 17 Then¦357014 Daniel¦357015 went¦357017 \add back\add* to¦357016 his house¦357016 and told his companions Hananyah¦357018, Misha'el¦357019, and Azaryah¦357020
\v 18 \add ≈so\add* they¦357038 \add ≈might beg\add* for mercy from¦357027 the god¦357030 of the heavens¦357031 concerning¦357032 the mystery¦357034 \add of the dream\add*, so that¦357035 \add ≈all of them\add* might not¦357037 be executed along with¦357041 the rest¦357043 of the Babylonian wise¦357044 men¦357044.
\v 19 Then the mystery¦357053 was revealed¦357054 to¦357048 Daniel in¦357049 a vision¦357049 during the night¦357052. Aftewrwards, Daniel blessed¦357057 the god¦357058 of the heavens¦357059
\v 20 saying¦357063, “Let¦357062 God's¦357068 name¦357065 be blessed¦357069 forever and ever, because \add all\add* wisdom¦357077 and power¦357078 belong to¦357080 him¦357080.
\v 21 He moves the times¦357086 and seasons¦357087 forwards. He removes¦357088 \add some\add* kings¦357089 and \add ≈gives power to others\add*. It's him who gives wisdom¦357093 to the wise¦357094 and knowledge¦357095 to those who have understanding.
\v 22 He reveals¦357100 the deep¦357101 and hidden¦357102 things¦357101—he¦357103 knows¦357103 what is in¦357105 the darkness¦357105, and the light¦357106 lives with¦357108 him¦357108.
\v 23 Oh¦357132 God¦357113 of¦357113 my¦357114 ancestors¦357114, I¦357117 thank you and praise you for the wisdom¦357119 and power¦357120 you have¦357121 given¦357121 to me. Now¦357123 you've revealed to me what we¦357127 asked¦357127 you about¦357113 because you've told us what the king¦357132 is wanting to know.”
\s1 Daniel explains the king's dream
\rem /s1 Daniel Tells the King the Dream and Explains It; Daniel Interprets the Dream
\p
\v 24 So Daniel¦357139 went¦357140 to Aryok¦357143, the man that¦357138 the king had¦357145 appointed¦357145 to execute Babylon's¦357149 wise men, and told him, “Don't¦357158 hurt the wise men in Babylon. Take me¦357161 in to the king and I'll¦357166 tell \add *him\add* the \add dream and its\add* interpretation¦357164.”
\p
\v 25 Aryok¦357170 quickly took Daniel¦357173 in to¦357173 the king¦357175 and¦357176 told him¦357179, “I've¦357182 found¦357182 a man¦357183 among the exiles¦357187 from Yehudah¦357189 who will reveal the interpretation¦357191 \add of your dream\add*.”
\p
\v 26 “Are¦357202 you able¦357204 to¦357205 tell me¦357205 the dream¦357206 that¦357207 I¦357209 saw¦357209, and¦357210 its¦357210 interpretation¦357210?” the king¦357196 \add ≈asked\add* Daniel¦357198 (also called Belteshatstsar).
\p
\v 27 “No wise¦357223 men¦357223, enchanters, magicians¦357225, or astrologers¦357226 are¦357227 able¦357227 to do what the¦357229 king¦357229 has demanded,” replied Daniel¦357213.
\v 28 “However, there¦357232 is¦357233 a god¦357233 in the heavens¦357234 who reveals¦357235 mysteries, and he's¦357237 the one who's shown you, King¦357238 Nevukadnetstsar¦357239, what¦357240 will¦357242 happen in the days¦357244 \add ≈to come\add*. This was your dream¦357245 and the visions¦357246 in your mind while you were in your bed¦357250:
\v 29 Oh¦357258 king¦357258, \add ≈as you lay there\add* your thoughts¦357259 turned to what would happen \add ≈in the future\add*, and¦357269 the one who reveals¦357269 mysteries¦357270 showed you what is going to happen.
\v 30 As for me, this¦357290 mystery¦357289 was revealed to me, not because I'm wiser than \add ≈anyone else\add*, but¦357277 so that the interpretation¦357298 could be revealed to the king¦357299, and¦357277 so that you can \add ≈understand what you saw in¦357279\add* your¦357302 mind¦357302.
\p
\v 31 “What you were¦357310 looking at, \add ≈your¦357321 majesty\add*, was an¦357312 incredible¦357314, huge¦357314 statue¦357315. It was heavy and¦357318 \add ≈shone brightly\add* as it stood¦357320 \add there\add* \add ≈in front¦357321 of¦357318\add* you—a terrifying¦357323 sight.
\v 32 The¦357326 statue's¦357326 head¦357327 was¦357328 made of pure gold¦357330, its chest¦357332 and arms¦357333 were silver¦357335, its belly¦357336 and thighs¦357337 were bronze¦357339,
\v 33 its legs were made of iron, and its feet¦357344 were \add ≈a combination\add* of iron and clay¦357352.
\v 34 You¦357355 continued looking until¦357356 a stone¦357359 was¦357358 \add ≈supernaturally\add* cut¦357358 out¦357358 \add from a mountain\add*, and it \add tumbled down and\add* struck¦357364 the¦357365 statue¦357365 on¦357363 its¦357368 feet¦357368 of¦357369 iron¦357370 and clay¦357371, and it smashed them¦357373 to pieces.
\v 35 Then the iron¦357378, the clay¦357379, the bronze¦357380, the silver¦357381, and the gold¦357382 were crushed all at the same time and became like¦357384 the \add ≈small pieces of dry straw at harvest time in\add* the summer¦357389. Then the wind¦357392 carried¦357390 them away so that no trace of them could be found¦357398, \add ≈but¦357382\add* the stone¦357400 that struck¦357404 the statue¦357405 became a¦357407 huge¦357408 mountain¦357407 that then¦357375 filled¦357409 the whole¦357393 world.
\p
\v 36 “That¦357420 was the dream¦357415, and¦357416 \add now\add* we'll¦357417 tell¦357417 the king¦357420 its¦357416 meaning:
\v 37 You¦357425, \add ≈your majesty\add*, are¦357426 the king¦357426 of kings¦357427 who has¦357435 been given¦357435 the kingdom¦357431, the power¦357432, the strength¦357433, and the splendour by the god¦357429 of the heavens¦357430.
\v 38 Wherever people live, he's \add ≈placed you in charge of¦357453\add* them, \add and\add* he's \add even\add* put you over¦357455 the animals¦357447 in the countryside¦357448 and the birds in the skies¦357451, \add so\add* you're¦357456 the \add statue's\add* gold¦357463 head¦357461.
\v 39 \add ≈But¦357465 another less prominent kingdom will follow yours\add*, then¦357465 another kingdom of¦357478 bronze¦357479 to rule¦357481 over all¦357482 the¦357484 earth¦357484 \add after that\add*.
\v 40 Then¦357486 there'll be¦357489 a fourth¦357487 kingdom¦357486 that's strong¦357490 as iron—because¦357494 iron crushes¦357503 and¦357486 shatters¦357497 all things. Like an iron \add sledge-hammer\add* that smashes things into pieces, it will smash all those¦357506 others into \add ≈broken\add* pieces.
\v 41 And as you saw the feet¦357513 and toes¦357514, \add ≈a mixture of iron and potter's¦357521 clay\add*, it will be a divided¦357526 kingdom¦357525—some of the iron's¦357532 strength¦357530 will be in¦357535 it, just as you saw iron mixed¦357542 with¦357535 the soft clay.
\v 42 As the feet¦357547 were¦357550 partly iron¦357550 and partly clay¦357553, so that kingdom¦357557 will¦357558 be¦357558 partly strong¦357559 and partly \add ≈weak\add*.
\v 43 As¦357583 you¦357566 saw¦357566 the¦357567 iron mixed¦357568 with soft clay, so \add ≈various peoples will¦357572 make alliances with each other\add*, but¦357575 they¦357577 won't¦357575 \add ≈stick together\add* just as iron doesn't \add ≈integrate\add* with clay.
\v 44 In¦357593 the days¦357593 of those kings¦357595, the god¦357598 of the heavens¦357599 will establish a kingdom¦357605 that¦357601 will never be¦357609 destroyed¦357604 or \add ≈taken over by others\add*. It will crush all¦357612 those kingdoms¦357615 and¦357593 put¦357611 an¦357611 end¦357611 to¦357611 them, \add ≈but¦357593\add* it¦357617 will stand forever¦357618.
\v 45 Just as you¦357625 saw¦357625 that a stone¦357629 was¦357628 \add ≈supernaturally\add* cut¦357628 out¦357628 of the mountain¦357627, and that it¦357634 crushed¦357634 the iron¦357635, the bronze¦357636, the clay¦357637, the silver¦357638, and the gold¦357639, the \add ≈powerful\add* God¦357640 has¦357642 revealed to¦357643 the king¦357643 what¦357644 will¦357646 happen \add ≈in¦357633 the future\add*. The dream¦357650 is true, and its¦357652 interpretation¦357652 is trustworthy¦357651.”
\s1 Daniel is rewarded by the king
\rem /s1 Daniel and His Friends Promoted; The King Rewards Daniel; Nebuchadnezzar Rewards Daniel
\p
\v 46 Then¦357655 King¦357656 Nevukadnetstsar¦357657 fell¦357658 \add ≈to his knees with his face¦357661 to the ground to worship\add* Daniel¦357662, and he commanded that a \add burnt\add* offering¦357664 and incense¦357665 be offered¦357667 up to him¦357668.
\v 47 The¦357671 king¦357671 said to Daniel¦357672, “It's true that¦357690 your god¦357678 is¦357680 the god of gods¦357681 and the master¦357682 of masters, and the one¦357684 who¦357684 reveals¦357684 mysteries, because you've¦357687 been¦357687 able¦357687 to reveal¦357688 this¦357690 mystery¦357689.”
\v 48 Then the king¦357693 gave Daniel¦357694 high honours and gave him many great gifts¦357696, and he¦357702 made¦357702 him ruler¦357702 over the whole¦357704 province¦357706 of Babylon and chief¦357708 supervisor over all¦357712 of Babylon's¦357707 wise¦357714 men¦357714.
\v 49 Daniel \add ≈asked¦357718 the king to appoint¦357722\add* Shadrak¦357728 \add !(Hananyah¦357728)\add*, Meyshak¦357728 \add !(Misha'el¦357728)\add*, and¦357717 Avednego \add !(Azaryah)\add* over¦357723 the administration¦357724 of the Babylonian province¦357726, \add ≈but¦357717\add* Daniel remained at¦357733 the king's¦357734 court¦357733.
\c 3
\s1 The command to worship the statue
\rem /s1 Nebuchadnezzar Commands Everyone to Worship a Gold Statue; The fiery furnace; Nebuchadnezzar's Gold Statue; The Image of Gold and the Fiery Furnace; The Golden Image
\p
\v 1 King¦357738 Nevukadnetstsar \add !(Nebuchadnezzar¦357737)\add* had a gold¦357743 statue made¦357739 that stood nearly thirty metres high and was almost three metres wide, and it¦357750 was erected on¦357751 the Dura¦357752 plain¦357751 in¦357751 the Babylonian province¦357753.
\v 2 Then¦357756 King¦357757 Nevukadnetstsar¦357776 sent¦357758 for the \add ≈various levels of\add* governors¦357763, the counsellors¦357764, the treasurers¦357765, the judges¦357766, the magistrates¦357767, and¦357756 all the provincial¦357770 officials, to all come¦357771 to the dedication¦357772 of the statue¦357773 that¦357774 \add *he'd\add* erected.
\v 3 \add ≈So all¦357788 those officials assembled in front¦357799 of¦357789 the statue that¦357793 the king¦357796 had¦357794 set¦357794 up¦357794, ready\add* for¦357791 the dedication¦357791.
\v 4 Then¦357805 the¦357805 herald¦357805 loudly proclaimed¦357806, “You¦357810 peoples¦357810, nations¦357811, and¦357812 languages¦357812 are¦357809 commanded
\v 5 that any time¦357814 you hear¦357817 the sound¦357818 of¦357818 the horn¦357819, flute¦357820, lyre, harps, pipes, and \add ≈other instruments\add*, you all¦357826 must¦357817 bow down¦357829 and worship¦357830 the gold¦357832 statue¦357831 that King¦357836 Nevukadnetstsar¦357835 has¦357834 erected.
\v 6 \add ≈Anyone who refuses\add* to bow down and¦357844 worship¦357844 \add >it¦357845\add* at¦357845 that time, will be¦357848 thrown¦357848 into a blazing¦357853 furnace¦357851.”
\v 7 \add ≈So when all those people¦357866 who had¦357887 gathered heard¦357863 the sounds¦357867 made by the musical¦357876 instruments\add*, they¦357883 all bowed down¦357877 to¦357884 worship the gold¦357885 statue¦357884.
\s1 Daniel's three friends get tattled on
\rem /s1 Daniel's Three Friends Are Accused of Disobedience
\p
\v 8 \add ≈But¦357901\add* some Chaldean¦357900 \add astrologers\add* \add ≈went to the king to make trouble for\add* the Jews¦357904.
\v 9 They¦357907 reported to¦357908 King¦357909 Nevukadnetstsar¦357908, “Long¦357910 live¦357912 the king.
\v 10 Oh¦357916 king¦357916, you've made¦357917 a decree¦357918 that¦357919 every¦357920 man who¦357923 hears¦357925 the sound¦357926 of the \add ≈musical¦357937 instruments\add*, must¦357925 bow down¦357938 and¦357939 worship¦357939 the gold¦357941 statue¦357940,
\v 11 \add ≈but¦357943\add* anyone¦357943 who doesn't bow and¦357949 worship¦357949 will be¦357950 thrown¦357950 into a fiery furnace¦357953.
\v 12 \add Now,\add* there¦357957 are¦357958 certain Jews who you've appointed¦357962 over¦357964 the administration¦357966 of the province¦357967 of Babylon¦357968: Shadrak¦357969, Meyshak, and¦357986 Avednego. These men, \add ≈your majesty\add*, \add ≈don't take any notice of\add* you: they¦357991 don't serve your gods¦357982, or worship the gold¦357987 statue¦357986 that¦357988 you've set¦357989 up¦357989.”
\p
\v 13 Then Nevukadnetstsar¦357995 in¦357996 a furious¦357997 rage¦357996 commanded that Shadrak¦358000 \add !(Hananyah¦358000)\add*, Meyshak¦358000 \add !(Misha'el¦358000)\add*, and¦357997 Avednego \add !(Azaryah)\add* be \add ≈arrested\add*. Then \add *they¦358007\add* were¦358007 brought¦358007 to the¦358009 king¦358009
