\v 15 Now if you're ready¦358034, as soon as you hear¦358039 the \add ≈musical¦358052 instruments\add* of¦358040 music¦358052, if you all¦358049 bow down¦358053 and¦358054 worship¦358054 the statue¦358055 I¦358058 \add ≈erected\add*, \add ≈everything will¦358034 be okay\add*. But¦358048 if you don't¦358060, you'll immediately be thrown¦358065 into a blazing¦358070 hot furnace¦358068. \add ≈Then what god¦358074 could rescue¦358077 you from¦358078 my¦358080 punishment\add*?”
\p
\v 16 “Oh¦358089 Nevukadnetstsar¦358089,” \add *the three of them\add* answered¦358087 the king¦358088, “we¦358093 don't¦358090 need to answer \add that last question\add*.
\v 17 \add ≈If¦358117 that's what happens to us\add*, the god¦358102 that we¦358105 serve is able¦358107 to rescue¦358108 us from the blazing¦358113 fire¦358112 \add ≈and¦358114 from your¦358116 punishment. He¦358118 will¦358118 save us,\add* \add ≈your majesty\add*.
\v 18 \add ≈But¦358126 just so it's¦358123 clear\add* \add ≈your majesty\add*, even if he doesn't, we won't¦358121 serve your gods¦358128 or worship that¦358138 gold¦358137 statue¦358136.”
\s1 Shadrak, Meyshak, and Avednego thrown into the fire
