\p
\v 19 \add ≈That made Nevukadnetstsar¦358145 absolutely furious and it could be seen in his face¦358149\add*. He¦358158 ordered that the furnace¦358161 be made seven¦358164 times hotter \add ≈than usual\add*
\v 20 and commanded \add ≈some of his¦358175 top warriors¦358170\add* to tie up Shadrak¦358178, Meyshak, and Avednego and then¦358170 to throw¦358182 them into the blazing¦358185 furnace¦358183.
\v 21 \add ≈So while still fully dressed even with¦358191 their¦358192 turbans¦358195 still on¦358191,\add* they¦358190 were¦358190 tied¦358190 up and thrown¦358197 into the middle of the furnace¦358200 where the fire¦358201 was blazing¦358202.
\v 22 \add Unfortunately\add*, because¦358206 of¦358211 the king's¦358212 insistent command, the furnace¦358214 was¦358215 extremely hot and¦358214 the flames¦358227 killed¦358225 the men¦358217 who¦358219 \add ≈threw them¦358226 in\add*,
\v 23 \add ≈but¦358231\add* Shadrak¦358234, Meyshak, and¦358236 Avednego fell¦358238 into the \add ≈roaring flames¦358243 while still securely tied¦358245 up\add*.
\p
\v 24 Then¦358248 King¦358250 Nevukadnetstsar¦358249 was¦358251 startled and jumped up and asked his¦358256 counsellors¦358256, “Wasn't¦358269 it three¦358259 \add ≈who were tied¦358264 up\add* that were thrown¦358260 \add there\add* into the middle of¦358261 the fire¦358263?
\p “Certainly¦358268, \add ≈your majesty\add*,” they replied¦358266.
\p
\v 25 “Look¦358273 \add there, then\add*!” he¦358271 responded. “I¦358275 can see¦358276 \em four¦358278\em* men¦358277 walking¦358280 in¦358281 the middle of¦358281 the fire¦358283, and they're not tied up and they're not hurt¦358284, and the fourth¦358291 one looks¦358273 like¦358293 a \add ≈young god\add*.”
\rem /s1 The Three Men Are Released and Promoted
\p
\v 26 Then Nevukadnetstsar¦358301 went over by the furnace¦358303 door¦358302 and \add ≈called out\add*, “Shadrak, Meyshak, and Avednego, servants¦358313 of the Most¦358317 High¦358317 God¦358316, come out, and come over here!” Then \add *the three men\add* walked out from¦358327 the middle of the fire¦358330.
\v 27 \add ≈All the king's¦358337 officials\add* gathered¦358332 \add ≈around¦358332 them\add* and saw¦358338 that¦358341 the fire¦358345 \add ≈hadn't harmed them\add*—their hair¦358347 wasn't even singed¦358350, their cloaks weren't damaged, and \add ≈they¦358353 didn't¦358342 even smell¦358354 like smoke\add*.
\p
\v 28 Nevukadnetstsar¦358361 \add ≈spoke\add*, “\add ≈Praise\add* the god of Shadrak¦358367, Meyshak, and Avednego. He¦358375 sent¦358373 his angel and rescued¦358375 his servants¦358376 who¦358377 \add ≈had put their trust¦358378\add* in him¦358379. They¦358378 \add ≈took objection to\add* the king's¦358381 command¦358380 \add ≈at the cost of their own lives\add*, rather than serve or worship any¦358394 god except¦358397 their god.
\v 29 I \add ≈hereby\add* make a decree¦358402 that any¦358404 people, nation¦358407, or language¦358408 that speaks anything offensive against¦358414 the god¦358415 of¦358416 Shadrak¦358418, Meyshak, and Avednego will have their¦358415 houses¦358424 made¦358423 into \add #rubbish heaps\add* and they'll \add ≈be¦358426 torn limb from limb\add*, because¦358429 there's¦358432 no other god who's¦358435 able¦358437 to¦358438 save \add people\add* like¦358439 that.”
\p
\v 30 Then¦358441 the king¦358442 \add ≈gave\add* Shadrak¦358444 \add !(Hananyah¦358444)\add*, Meyshak¦358444 \add !(Misha'el¦358444)\add*, and¦358446 Avednego \add !(Azaryah)\add* \add ≈more important positions in¦358448 Babylon¦358449 province¦358448 than they had before\add*.
\c 4
\s1 Nevukadnetstsar's second dream
\rem /s1 Nebuchadnezzar's Dream of a Tree; Nebuchadnezzar's Second Dream; Nebuchadnezzar's madness; Nebuchadnezzar's Dream about a Tree
\p
\v 1 King¦358501 Nevukadnetstsar¦358501 \add !(Nebuchadnezzar¦358501)\add* \add sent this message\add* to all people groups, nations, and languages that live all over the world:
\p May you \add ≈have peace and prosperity¦358505\add*.
\v 2 It¦358511 seemed good to me to \add ≈let you all know about¦358515\add* the \add ≈amazing miracles\add* that the \add ≈supreme\add* God¦358510 has done for me.
\q1
\v 3 The miracles he does are incredible,
\q1 ≈and his acts of creation are powerful.
\q1 His kingdom will¦358533 last forever,
\q1 ≈and his authority goes from one generation to¦358533 another.
\p
\v 4 I¦358546, Nevukadnetstsar¦358541, was¦358545 \add ≈doing well at home\add*, and¦358548 prospering \add at my work\add* in my palace,
\v 5 \add ≈but¦358555\add* \add one night\add* as I lay in bed, I had a dream¦358572 that scared me—the images and¦358566 visions in my head terrified me.
\v 6 \add ≈So I¦358582 summoned\add* all of Babylon's wise men to come and tell¦358602 me the interpretation¦358601 of the dream¦358597.
\v 7 \add ≈When\add* those magicians, fortune-tellers, sorcerers and astrologers arrived, I¦358611 told them the dream, but¦358605 they didn't know the interpretation.
\p
\v 8 Finally, Daniel came in—he'd been named Belteshatstsar \add !(Belteshazzar)\add* after my god's name. The¦358621 spirit of¦358623 the holy gods is in him, and¦358626 I told him the dream:
\v 9 “Oh Belteshatstsar, chief of the magicians, since I know that the spirit of the holy gods is in¦358640 you and that no mystery is too difficult for you, \add ≈go through\add* the visions of my dream and tell me their interpretation.
\p
\v 10 This is the vision¦358660 that was in my mind as a lay in bed¦358664: I¦358659 was looking and was surprised to see¦358665 \add ≈a very tall\add* tree growing in the middle of \add ≈a field\add*.
\v 11 The¦358679 tree¦358679 kept growing taller and stronger \add ≈until\add* its top reached the sky and it¦358686 \add ≈could be seen from¦358688 all over the world\add*.
\v 12 It had beautiful leaves¦358701 were beautiful and lots of fruit on¦358700 it. It \add provided\add* food for everything, the animals¦358715 in the countryside¦358709 found shade under it, and the birds of the sky lived in its branches. \add ≈Every living thing benefitted\add* from it.
\p
\v 13 I was looking at the visions in my mind¦358721 as I lay in bed, and¦358727 then, wow, a holy sentinel came down from the heavens.
\v 14 He shouted loudly, ‘Chop that tree down and cut its branches off, strip off its leaves, and scatter its fruit. Let the animals \add ≈run away\add* from under it and the birds from its branches.
\v 15 But¦358776 leave the stump with its¦358779 roots in the ground, and bind it with a band of iron and bronze. Let it become wet with the dew in the tender grass of the field, \add ≈accompanied only by those animals that¦358770 live\add* in the grass.
\v 16 Let his mind\f + \fr 4:16 \ft It seems now that the tree represented a man.\f* be changed from a man to an animal as seven periods pass over him¦358817.
\v 17 \add &That\add* sentence is by the decree of the sentinels, and the decision is a command of the holy ones, so that the living may know that the highest one is ruler over human \add #kingdoms\add* and gives \add #them\add* to whoever he wants—even to those \add ≈who seem unimportant\add*.’
\p
\v 18 I, King Nevukadnetstsar, had this dream. Now you, Belteshatstsar, tell me the interpretation, because none of the wise men in my kingdom can tell me the interpretation. But¦358855 you can, because the spirit of the holy gods is in you.”
\s1 The saving/explaining of Daniel of dream of King
\rem /s1 Daniel Explains the Dream; Daniel Interprets the Second Dream; Daniel Interprets the Dream
\p
\v 19 Daniel (also known as Belteshatstsar) was appalled for a while \add at the meaning of the dream\add*, and \add ≈worried how to present it\add*. The¦358884 king¦358877 \add saw this\add* and said, Belteshatstsar, don't \add ≈hold back on being honest about¦358881\add* the dream its interpretation.”
\p “My master,” \add *he\add* answered, “\add ≈If only\add* the¦358877 dream was for those who hate you, and¦358883 its interpretation was for your enemies.
\v 20 You¦358892 saw¦358891 that tree¦358902 that grew and became¦358919 strong, and whose top reached the sky and was visible all \add ≈over the world\add*,
\v 21 with its beautiful leaves and plentiful fruit. \add ≈It provided\add* food for everyone, and the animals from the countryside lived under it, and the birds lived in its branches.
\v 22 That was you¦358950, \add ≈your majesty\add*. You've grown and become strong—your greatness has¦358982 grown and reaches to heaven¦358967, and your dominion to the ends of¦358986 the earth.
\v 23 \add ≈Then\add* the king saw a holy sentinel coming down from heaven¦359011 and saying¦358996, ‘Chop down the tree¦359001 and destroy it, but¦358995 leave¦358997 the stump¦358998 and the roots¦358999 in the ground, bound with a band of iron and bronze, in the tender grass of the countryside, and let him be wet with the dew, and let \add ≈him live\add* with the animals of the fields until seven periods of time pass over him.’
\p
\v 24 “\add ≈Your majesty\add*, this is the interpretation: It's \add actually\add* the decree of what the supreme God has declared will happen to \add you\add*, my master the king¦359015.
\v 25 You will be driven \add ≈away from society\add* and you'll live with the animals in the countryside. You'll be forced to eat grass like a bull, and you'll \add sleep on the ground and\add* be wet with dew \add in the morning\add* for seven \add ≈years\add* until you acknowledge that the highest one is ruler over \add ≈humankind\add* and \add ≡learn that\add* he gives kingdoms to whoever he wants.
\v 26 \add ≈But¦359052\add* \add just\add* as it was commanded to leave the stump of the tree with its roots, \add so too\add* your kingdom¦359050 will be restored to you from the time you recognise that heaven rules \add over earth\add*.
\v 27 Therefore, \add ≈your majesty¦359075\add*, \add ≈please consider\add* my advice: \add ≈stop sinning and do what is¦359061 right\add*, and \add ≡stop\add* \add ≈disobeying God by starting to\add* show mercy to the oppressed, so that¦359066 perhaps your prosperity \add ≈might¦359072 be prolonged\add*.”
\rem /s1 The Dream's Fulfillment; Nebuchadnezzar's Humiliation; The Dream Is Fulfilled
\p
\v 28 \add But\add* all of¦359080 that did happen to¦359087 King¦359081 Nevukadnetstsar¦359089.
\v 29 Twelve months \add ≈later,\add* he was walking on¦359125 \add the upper terrace of\add* the royal palace in¦359125 Babylon
\v 30 \add ≈when\add* \add *he\add* \add looked across\add* and said, “Isn't that the great Babylon, which I've built as a royal residence by the strength of my power and for the glory of my majesty?”
\p
\v 31 \add ≈He'd only just finished saying that when he heard a voice in\add* the sky, “It's \add now\add* decreed to you, King Nebuchadnezzar¦359165: you've just lost your kingdom¦359184.
\v 32 You'll be driven away from \add other\add* people, and \add ≈you'll live\add* will be with¦359200 the animals \add out\add* in¦359200 the countryside \add where\add* you'll \add ≈have¦359214 to\add* eat grass like a cow. Seven \add ≈years will pass before\add* you acknowledge that the highest one is¦359207 ruler over mankind and he gives \add @kingdoms\add* to whoever he wants.”
\p
\v 33 Immediately \add ≈what had been said in¦359217 advance happened to\add* Nebuchadnezzar. He was driven away from \add ≈society\add*. He ate grass like a cow, and his body was wet with¦359217 dew \add each morning after sleeping on the ground\add*. \add ≈Eventually\add* his hair had grown long like eagles' feathers, and his nails \add ≡were long\add* like birds' claws.
\s1 Nevukadnetstsar praises God
\rem /s1 Nebuchadnezzar Praises God
\p
\v 34 “After \add the seven years\add*, I, Nevukadnetstsar¦359246 \add ≈looked up toward\add* heaven¦359251 and my reason returned to me. Then I praised¦359247 and honoured¦359249 the highest one who lives forever, because his authority is everlasting and his kingdom endures \add ≈through the generations\add*.
\v 35 \add Compared to him,\add* all the inhabitants of the earth are \add ≈insignificant like ants\add*—he \add ≈issues his commands\add* to the heavenly army and to earth's inhabitants. No one can hold back his hand or \add ≈has any right to ask\add* him, ‘What have you done?’ ”
\p
\v 36 “At that time my sanity returned to me, and I was reestablished in my kingdom. My majesty and my splendour was restored to me \add ≈as I became again the respected leader\add* of my kingdom. My counsellors and my nobles \add ≈wanted to be in my company\add*, and \add ≈I became even more powerful than I was before\add*.
\v 37 Now I, Nevukadnetstsar, praise, extol, and honour the king of the heavens, because \add ≈everything he does is\add*, and his \add ≈methods\add* are just, and he's able to humble those who \add ≈become proud\add*.
\c 5
\s1 The writing on the wall
\rem /s1 Belshazzar's Banquet; The writing on the wall; Belshazzar's Feast; The Writing on the Wall
\p
\v 1 \add Several years later when\add* Belshatstsar \add was\add* king¦359267, he put on a feast¦359269 for¦359271 a thousand of¦359271 his¦359271 nobles¦359271, and¦359273 he was drinking¦359276 wine¦359275 with them \add *all\add*.
\v 2 \add ≈As Belshatstsar was drinking\add* the wine¦359282, he \add remembered\add* the gold¦359285 and silver¦359286 cups that his father¦359290 Nevukadnetstsar¦359289 had¦359288 taken from the temple¦359293 in Yerushalem¦359295, \add and\add* ordered that they¦359296 be brought¦359288 in so¦359296 that the king¦359298 and his nobles¦359299, and his wives¦359300 and his concubines¦359301, could¦359296 drink¦359296 from them.
\v 3 \add ≈So¦359320\add* they¦359304 brought¦359304 in the gold¦359306 cups that¦359307 had¦359308 been taken out¦359308 of¦359314 God's¦359315 residence in Yerushalem¦359317, then¦359303 the king¦359320 and his nobles¦359321, his wives¦359322 and his concubines¦359323, drank¦359318 from them.
\v 4 They¦359325 drank¦359325 the wine¦359326 and praised¦359327 the gods¦359328 of¦359328 gold¦359329 and silver¦359330, bronze¦359331, iron¦359332, wood¦359333, and stone¦359334.
\rem /s1 The Writing on the Wall
\p
\v 5 Suddenly, the fingers¦359341 of a¦359343 human¦359345 hand appeared and¦359346 started writing on the plaster¦359351 on the king's¦359357 palace¦359355 wall¦359354, opposite the lampstand¦359348. \add When\add* the king saw¦359359 the back of the hand that¦359362 was¦359363 writing,
\v 6 he \add ≈was very frightened and the¦359372 colour drained from his face¦359367\add*. His¦359366 \add ≈legs felt weak\add* and his knees¦359375 started knocking¦359378 \add together\add*,
\v 7 \add and\add* \add *he\add* called¦359380 out for the enchanters¦359384, the Chaldeans, and the astrologers¦359387 to be brought¦359383 in. The¦359389 king¦359389 told those Babylonian wise men, “Anyone who¦359398 can read¦359400 this¦359402 writing¦359401 and tell me¦359404 its¦359403 interpretation¦359403 will be \add ≈dressed in royal robes\add* and will have a chain¦359407 of¦359415 gold¦359412 \add placed\add* around his¦359415 neck¦359415, and he'll¦359400 become the third¦359416 ruler in the kingdom¦359417.”
\v 8 \add ≈When¦359426\add* all¦359424 the king's wise¦359425 men¦359425 of the king \add ≈got there\add*, \add ≈none of them\add* could read¦359431 the writing¦359430 or \add ≈tell the king what it meant\add*.
\v 9 Then King¦359438 Belshatstsar became more afraid, his face¦359442 \add ≈went whiter still\add*, and¦359445 his nobles¦359445 \add also\add* \add ≈had no idea what to do\add*.
\p
\v 10 \add My now\add* the queen\f + \fr 5:10 \ft Possibly ‘the previous queen’, i.e., the queen mother (as the next verse displays her knowledge of past events).\f* had heard about¦359450 what the king and his¦359452 noblemen¦359452 had been saying¦359459, so she¦359455 came into the banquet¦359454 house¦359453 and said¦359459, “Long live¦359462 the king. Don't let \add ≈this get under your¦359467 skin\add*. Don't let \add ≈it drain you¦359465\add*.
\v 11 There's¦359472 a man in your kingdom¦359474 who¦359475 has the spirit¦359476 of the holy gods in him. In¦359480 the days¦359480 of your father, this man has illumination¦359482 and insight¦359483 and wisdom like¦359485 the wisdom of the gods. Your¦359500 father King¦359490 Nevukadnetstsar¦359491 appointed¦359498 him chief¦359493 of the magicians¦359494, sorcerers, Chaldeans¦359496, and astrologers¦359497,
\v 12 because¦359504 this Daniel had an¦359511 excellent spirit¦359506, knowledge¦359509, and insight¦359510 for interpreting¦359511 dreams¦359512, explaining¦359513 riddles¦359514, and solving problems. \add ≈So summon¦359529\add* Daniel who the king¦359522 named Belteshatstsar and he'll¦359531 tell \add >you\add* the interpretation¦359530.”
\rem /s1 Daniel Explains the Writing; The Writing on the Wall Interpreted
\p
\v 13 \add ≈So\add* Daniel was¦359536 brought in to the king \add ≈who\add* said to \add *him\add*, “\add So,\add* you're that Daniel—one of the exiles¦359553 that my father¦359559 the king brought from¦359560 Yehudah¦359555.
\v 14 I've¦359564 heard about¦359565 you—\add ≡heard\add* that the spirit¦359568 of¦359568 the gods¦359569 is in you, and that \add ≈you have¦359564\add* illumination¦359571 and insight¦359572 and excellent wisdom¦359573.
\v 15 Now¦359578 the wise¦359581 men¦359581 and enchanters¦359582 were brought¦359579 in \add ≈here\add* to read¦359587 this writing¦359585 \add on the wall\add* and \add ≈tell\add* me its¦359588 interpretation¦359588, but¦359578 they¦359592 couldn't \add ≈interpret¦359593 it\add*.
\v 16 But¦359598 I've¦359599 heard¦359599 about¦359600 you—that you're able to give¦359608 interpretations¦359607 and solve¦359610 problems¦359609. Now¦359611, if¦359612 you're able to read¦359616 \add &that\add* writing¦359615 and \add ≈tell me¦359618 what it means\add*, you'll be clothed¦359620 with¦359631 \add ≈kingly robes\add* and have¦359599 a chain¦359621 of¦359624 gold¦359626 \add placed\add* around your¦359629 neck¦359629, and you'll be \add made\add* the third\add -highest\add* ruler in¦359631 the kingdom¦359631.”
\p
\v 17 “\add ≈I'm not after\add* your gifts¦359641, and your rewards¦359644 \add ≈can be given¦359646 to someone¦359645 else,\add*” Daniel¦359637 \add ≈responded\add*. “But¦359638, I'll read¦359649 the writing¦359648 to the king¦359650 and \add ≡I'll\add* \add ≈tell you\add* the interpretation¦359651.
\p
\v 18 “As for you, \add ≈your majesty\add*, the highest god¦359657 gave¦359664 the kingship, power, honour¦359662, and majesty, to¦359665 your father¦359666 Nevukadnetstsar¦359665.
\v 19 Because of \add >that¦359671\add* power, all peoples¦359676, nations¦359677, and ethnic \add groups\add* trembled¦359680 and \add ≈were afraid¦359682 when his¦359685 army approached\add*. He killed¦359691 whoever he wanted, and he kept alive those he wanted. He \add ≈promoted\add* whoever he wanted, and he humiliated \add ≈others when he wanted to\add*.
\v 20 But¦359711 when \add ≈he became proud¦359716 and stubborn\add* and started acting¦359716 presumptuously¦359716, he was \add ≈disqualified\add* from¦359718 the¦359720 throne¦359720, and his \add ≈splendid reputation was deflated\add*.
\v 21 He was driven \add ≈right out of¦359731 the community\add*, and \add ≈he started behaving like\add* an animal¦359735—he \add ended up\add* living¦359742 with wild¦359741 donkeys¦359741 and eating grass¦359743 like a cow. \add He slept on the ground, and in the morning\add* his¦359748 body¦359748 was wet¦359749 with dew¦359746, until¦359750 he \add finally\add* recognized that the highest god¦359757 is ruler over \add ≈all\add* mankind¦359761 and that he \add ≈appoints¦359766 whoever he wants as leaders over the kingdoms¦359760\add*.
\p
\v 22 “Yet you¦359770, Belshatstsar, his¦359772 son¦359772, haven't humbled \add ≈yourself despite already knowing¦359784 all¦359781 that¦359783\add*.
\v 23 \add On the contrary\add*, you've¦359791 \add ≈proudly promoted\add* yourself¦359791 \add ≈as being greater than\add* the master¦359787 of¦359787 the heavens¦359789. \add ≈You brought¦359796 the temple cups in¦359808 here\add* and you and your nobles¦359801, your wives¦359804 and your concubines¦359805, have drunk¦359807 wine¦359806 from them¦359808. You praised¦359826 the gods¦359809 \add ≈made from\add* silver¦359810 and gold¦359812, bronze¦359813, iron¦359814, wood¦359815, and stone¦359816, which can't¦359818 see¦359820, or hear¦359823, or know¦359825 \add >anything\add*, yet you haven't honoured the god¦359827 \add ≈who¦359828 decides your future and every breath¦359830 that you take\add*.
\p
\v 24 “\add So\add* then¦359839 \add its\add* from¦359840 his presence¦359842 \add that\add* the¦359845 palm¦359845 of¦359846 a hand¦359848 was sent¦359843, and¦359849 this¦359850 \add ≈message was written¦359849\add*.
\v 25 This¦359853 \add ≈is¦359854 how it¦359856 reads\add*, ‘Numbered, numbered, weighed, and¦359860 divided,’
\v 26 \add and\add* this¦359862 is¦359863 what it¦359871 means:
\li1 \bd Numbered\bd*: God¦359869 has¦359867 numbered¦359867 \add ≈your¦359870 days of¦359870 leadership\add* and brought¦359871 \add #them\add* to an end¦359871,
\li1
\v 27 \bd Weighed\bd*: You've been¦359874 weighed¦359874 on¦359875 the¦359875 scales¦359875 and found¦359876 deficient, \add and\add*
\li1
\v 28 \bd Divided\bd*: Your kingdom¦359881 has been divided and given¦359882 to¦359883 \add the\add* Medes¦359883 and \add the\add* Persians.”
\p
\v 29 Then¦359886 Belshatstsar¦359889 \add ≈gave an order\add*, and they¦359890 \add ≈placed a purple¦359892 robe over Daniel's¦359891 shoulders\add* and a gold¦359897 chain¦359893 was \add ≈placed\add* around his¦359900 neck¦359900, and an \add official\add* announcement¦359901 was made¦359901 that¦359903 he¦359905 would \add now\add* be¦359905 the third\add -highest\add* ruler¦359906 in¦359908 the kingdom¦359908.
\p
\v 30 \add However,\add* the¦359911 Chaldean King¦359914 Belshatstsar was¦359912 killed¦359912 that \add ≈very\add* night¦359911,
\v 31 and Dareyavesh \add !(Darius)\add* the Mede \add ≈became the new ruler at an age of\add* around sixty-two.
\c 6
\s1 Daniel gets fed to the lions
\rem /s1 Daniel in the Den of Lions; The Plot against Daniel; Daniel in the lion-pit; Daniel in the Lions' Den; Daniel in the Pit of Lions
\p
\v 1 \add The new King\add* Dareyavesh¦359920 \add !(Darius¦359920)\add* \add ≈decided to divide the¦359925 kingdom¦359925 into 120 provinces with a governor over each one\add*.
\v 2 Over¦359936 them were three higher officials (Daniel¦359934 was one of them) that those governors were accountable to so that the king \add ≈wouldn't be ripped off\add*,
\v 3 \add ≈but¦359949\add* because Daniel¦359954 was exceptionally talented, he became noticed above¦359949 the \add other\add* high officials and governors, and the king¦359966 \add ≈decided\add* to appoint him over the whole kingdom.
\v 4 \add ≈That led\add* the high officials and the governors to try to find \add ≈some fault with\add* Daniel¦359974 with regard to \add ≈his administrative¦359980 duties\add*. However, they weren't able to find any \add ≈misdemeanours or corruption that¦359975 he could be accused of\add* because¦359984 he was \add ^honest, reliable, and diligent\add*.
\v 5 Then¦359999 these men \add ≈concluded\add*, “We won't¦360013 find any grounds for¦360006 \add making an\add* accusation against this Daniel¦360006 unless we find something against him¦360030 to¦360006 do with the¦360007 \add #laws\add* of¦360007 his god.”
\p
\v 6 \add ≈So\add* those¦360035 high officials and governors \add made a plan and\add* went as a group to the king and \add ≈made this suggestion\add*, “\add ≈Long live your majesty\add*, King Dareyavesh.
\v 7 All the high officials of the kingdom, the \add ≈various leaders\add*, the counsellors, and the governors have consulted together that the king should establish and enforce a law that for thirty days, anyone who makes a request of any god or \add ≡of any\add* man other than you, \add ≈your majesty\add*, shall be thrown into the lions' den.
\v 8 Now, \add ≈your majesty\add*, sign the document and establish¦360079 \add ≈this law\add* so that¦360084 it \add ≈can't then be¦360104 revoked according to¦360105\add* the law of¦360105 the Medes and Persians.”
\v 9 \add ≈So\add* King¦360110 Dareyavesh signed the document¦360114 and \add ≈it¦360125 became law¦360118\add*.
\rem /s1 Daniel in the Lions' Den
\v 10 \add ≈However although\add* Daniel knew that \add ≈that law had been passed\add*, he \add still\add* went into his house (with the windows in his \add ≈upper room\add* open toward Yerushalem), and he continued to kneel three times a day to pray and give thanks to God, \add just\add* as he'd \add ≈always done\add*.
\p
\v 11 Then¦360153 those \add ≈officials\add* went¦360146 as a group and found Daniel¦360139 \add ≈praying¦360163 to his god¦360166 and presenting him with¦360151 his requests\add*.
\v 12 Then¦360181 they approached the king and¦360185 \add ≈reminded him about¦360190 the law\add*, “Didn't you \add ≈introduce a law\add* that within thirty days, any man who makes a petition to any god¦360190 or man, except to you, \add ≈your majesty\add*, must be thrown into the lions' den?”
\p “\add ≈Yes, that's correct\add*,” answered the king. “It can't be revoked now according to the law of the Medes and Persians.”
\p
\v 13 Then¦360193 they \add ≈informed\add* the king, “Daniel who¦360210 was exiled from Yehudah, pays no attention to you, \add ≈your majesty\add*, or to the \add ≈law¦360234 that¦360238 you introduced\add*, because he petitions \add his god\add* three times a day.”
\p
\v 14 When the¦360248 king¦360248 heard \add ≈that\add*, was very upset and¦360246 \add ≈for that entire day¦360272, he¦360260 actively tried to think of a way to get Daniel¦360250 out of it\add*.
\v 15 \add ≈However, the group of men returned\add* to the king and said, “You know, \add ≈your majesty\add*, that it's a law of the Medes and Persians, that no rule or law that the king establishes can be changed.”
\p
\v 16 \add ≈So\add* the king gave an order, and Daniel was brought in and the king responded¦360305 and told \add *him\add*, “May your god, who you serve continually, save you.” Then Daniel was thrown \add down\add* into the lions' den.
\v 17 A stone was brought¦360331 and laid over the opening of the den¦360334, and the king¦360338 sealed it with his own signet ring and with the signet rings of his nobles so that \add ≈no one could¦360350 sneak Daniel¦360332 out\add*.
\v 18 Then the king¦360362 went to his palace and spent the night \add ≈without eating\add*. \add ≈He refused all\add* entertainment, and \add ≈he couldn't sleep\add*.
\s2 Daniel's saved from the lions
\rem /s1 Daniel Saved from the Lions
\p
\v 19 Then¦360376 the king¦360376 got up at dawn, and \add ≈hurried out\add* to the lions' den.
\v 20 As he \add ≈approached\add* the den¦360396, he called out in¦360392 a worried voice, “Daniel, Daniel, servant of the living god. Has your god, who you serve continually, been able to¦360396 save you from the lions¦360399?”
\p
\v 21 “\add ≈Long live¦360416\add* the king¦360410,” Daniel¦360405 \add ≈called back\add*.
\v 22 “My god sent his angel and shut the¦360436 lions' mouths, and they haven't hurt me, because \add ≈he¦360437 saw that I wasn't guilty of anything\add*. Also, \add ≈your majesty\add*, I haven't \add ≈wronged you in any way\add*.”
\p
\v 23 Then the king¦360462 was very happy, and ordered that Daniel be pulled up out of the den. So Daniel was lifted out of the den, and \add ≈he didn't¦360449 even have¦360465 a scratch\add* on him¦360455, because¦360453 he had trusted in his god¦360443.
\v 24 \add Then\add* the king¦360469 \add ≈ordered the arrest of\add* the men who had maliciously accused Daniel¦360473, and \add ≈had them and their wives and children thrown\add* into the lions' den—they didn't¦360487 \add even\add* reach the bottom of the den before the lions overpowered them and \add ≈tore them to pieces\add*.
\p
\v 25 Then¦360497 King¦360497 Dareyavesh¦360497 \add !(Darius¦360497)\add* wrote to all the peoples, nations, and \add ≈ethnic groups right across his¦360504 kingdom\add*: “May¦360506 you all \add ≈be enjoying peace and prosperity\add*.
\v 26 I decree that¦360539 \add ≈everyone¦360543 throughout\add* my kingdom is to \add ≈obey and respect\add* Daniel's god, because he is the \add ≈eternal\add*, living¦360541 God. His kingdom \add ≈will never\add* be destroyed, and his \add ≈rule will continue forever\add*.
\v 27 He delivers and rescues, and he does \add ≈all kinds of miracles\add* in the heavens and on earth. He's rescued Daniel¦360571 from the \add ≈strength and power\add* of the lions.”
\p
\v 28 \add ≈So\add* Daniel¦360600 prospered during the reign of Dareyavesh and¦360597 during the reign of Koresh \add !(Cyrus)\add* the Persian.
\c 7
\ms1 Daniel's visions
\mr (7:1–12:13)
\s1 Daniel's vision of four creatures
\rem /s1 Daniel's Vision of Four Beasts; Daniel's Vision of the Four Beasts; Visions of the Four Beasts; Daniel's visions; Daniel's Dream of Four Beasts
\p
\v 1 \add Previously,\add* in Babylonian King¦360621 Belshatstsar's first year¦360618, Daniel¦360623 had seen¦360625 a dream and visions¦360626 in his mind while lying on¦360628 his bed¦360630. Then¦360631 he wrote¦360633 the dream down and \add ≈gave a summary of¦360634 it\add*.
\v 2 \add *He¦360639\add* \add ≈explained\add*:
\p In¦360643 my¦360643 vision¦360643 at¦360643 night¦360646 I¦360642 was looking, and¦360647 \add ≈suddenly\add*, the four¦360648 winds¦360649 were¦360651 stirring¦360651 up¦360651 the massive ocean,
\v 3 and¦360655 four¦360655 huge¦360657 creatures, each one different¦360662 from¦360659 the¦360661 other, were¦360662 coming¦360658 up¦360658 out of \add *it\add*.\x + \xo 7:3: \xt Rev 13:1; 17:8.\x*
\li1
\v 4 The first¦360668 \add one\add* was like a lion¦360669 but¦360670 had the wings of¦360671 an¦360673 eagle¦360673. \add ≈But¦360670 as I¦360676 watched, its¦360681 wings were torn\add* off¦360680, and it was lifted¦360682 \add ≈up\add* and made¦360690 to stand¦360690 on¦360686 two¦360688 feet¦360688 like a man, and then it was given¦360693 a \add ≈human¦360692\add* mind¦360691.\x + \xo 7:4-6: \xt Rev 13:2.\x*
\li1
\v 5 The second¦360699 creature resembled a bear¦360701 \add ≈with¦360708 one arm raised¦360705\add*. \add ≈It was chomping on\add* three¦360706 ribs¦360707 and it was told¦360713 to go and \add ≈gorge itself\add* on \add more\add* meat¦360717.
\li1
\v 6 \add ≈Next I¦360723 saw¦360722 another¦360725 creature that¦360721 resembled\add* a leopard¦360726, \add ≈but¦360724\add* with four wings \add ≈(like¦360726 birds' wings) attached to\add* its¦360735 back¦360735, and¦360737 with four heads¦360738. \add ≈It¦360741 was¦360741 given¦360741 the¦360739 authority to rule people\add*.
\li1
\v 7 The¦360748 final creature that I¦360747 saw¦360746 in¦360748 the vision¦360748 was dreadful¦360755 and terrifying¦360754. It was extremely strong¦360756 with¦360748 large¦360763 iron¦360761 teeth¦360758 and it was devouring¦360764 \add >things\add* then trampling¦360769 and crushing¦360765 the remainder¦360766. \add ≈Another difference¦360771 from¦360772 the other creatures was that it had ten¦360780 horns¦360779 on¦360748 its¦360767 head\add*.\x + \xo 7:7: \xt Rev 12:3; 13:1; 17:3.\x*
\p
\v 8 While I¦360784 was¦360784 considering¦360783 the horns, \add ≈suddenly\add* another¦360788 horn¦360787, a little one, came¦360790 up¦360790 among¦360791 \add ≈the others\add* and three¦360793 of the first¦360797 horns \add ≈that were¦360798 in¦360785 its way got pulled completely out\add*. \add ≈Incredibly\add*, this \add new\add* had eyes¦360805 like¦360806 a person and a mouth¦360811 \add ≈that had a lot of incredible¦360813 things to say\add*.\x + \xo 7:8,11: \xt Rev 13:5.\x*
\rem /s1 The revelation of God concerning the one not die
\rem /s1 Judgment before the Ancient One; The Vision of the One Who Has Been Living Forever
\q1
\v 9 I¦360816 kept looking \add in the vision\add* while thrones¦360819 were set up and the ancient¦360821 \add ≈one\add* took his seat. His clothing¦360824 was as white¦360827 as snow¦360826, and his hair¦360828 \add looked\add* like¦360830 pure wool. His throne¦360832 was \add ≈flaming with\add* fire, \add and\add* its¦360837 wheels¦360837 were burning¦360839 fire.\x + \xo 7:9: a \xt Rev 20:4; \xo b \xt Rev 1:14.\x*
\v 10 A river¦360841 of fire¦360844 was¦360845 coming¦360846 out and flowing¦360845 \add ≈along in front of\add* him. Thousands¦360851 and thousands¦360851 served¦360853 him, and myriads upon myriads were standing¦360858 \add ≈in front of\add* him. The¦360859 court¦360859 sat¦360860 \add in session\add* and the documents were opened¦360862.\x + \xo 7:10: a \xt Rev 5:11; \xo b \xt Rev 20:12.\x*
\p
\v 11 Then¦360866 I continued looking because of¦360869 the \add ≈amazing things\add* that the horn¦360873 was saying. I kept looking until¦360877 that \add final\add* creature was killed¦360879 and its¦360882 body¦360882 destroyed¦360881 \add ≈in\add* the fire¦360885.
\v 12 As for the other creatures, their¦360890 \add ≈authority to rule\add* was taken away, but¦360887 an extension¦360891 of life¦360892 was given¦360893 to them¦360894 for a season and¦360887 a time.
\p
\v 13 I¦360901 was looking in¦360902 the visions¦360902 of¦360902 the night¦360903, and \add ≈suddenly\add*, \add ≈someone like¦360909 a human male\add* was coming¦360911 with¦360902 the clouds¦360907, and he came¦360911 to the ancient¦360915 \add ≈one\add* and was presented¦360919 to him.\x + \xo 7:13: \xt Mat 24:30; 26:64; Mrk 13:26; 14:62; Luk 21:27; Rev 1:7,13; 14:14.\x*
\v 14 He was given¦360922 \add ≈authority to rule\add* and \add ≈splendour\add* and a kingdom, so that¦360935 all¦360926 the peoples¦360927, nations¦360928, and \add ≈ethnic groups\add* will serve him¦360921. His¦360932 \add ≈authority will never end\add*, and his kingdom \add ≈will never\add* be¦360943 destroyed¦360943.\x + \xo 7:14: \xt Rev 11:15.\x*
\s1 The meaning of the visions
\rem /s1 Daniel's Visions Interpreted; The Interpretation of the Dream; The Visions Are Explained; The Vision Is Explained
\p
\v 15 As¦360948 for me, Daniel¦360949, my spirit¦360947 was¦360946 distressed¦360946 inside¦360950 me, and¦360952 the visions¦360952 in¦360950 my \add ≈mind\add* kept alarming¦360954 me.
\v 16 I approached¦360956 one¦360959 of those who were standing¦360962 there and asked¦360964 him¦360966 \add ≈what was¦360964 going on\add*, so he told me, and then he revealed the¦360975 interpretation¦360975 of those things¦360976 to¦360977 me:
\mi
\v 17 Those four large¦360981 creatures, they represent four \add ≈kingdoms\add* that¦360982 will¦360987 \add ≈come to be in the¦360990 world\add*,
\v 18 \add ≈but¦360992\add* the \add ≈dedicated followers\add* of the highest one will receive¦360992 the kingdom, and¦360992 they will \add ≈take¦360996 possession¦360996 of\add* the kingdom forever¦361003.
\p
\v 19 \add ≈But¦361027 I¦361006 still wanted to¦361007 know¦361007 more\add* about¦361008 the fourth¦361011 creature that¦361012 was different¦361015 from¦361016 all¦361018 the others, absolutely dreadful, with its iron¦361026 teeth¦361022 and bronze¦361030 claws¦361027, and which¦361012 devoured¦361031, then crushed and trampled¦361036 down with its feet¦361034 what was left.
\v 20 \add ≡I also wanted to know\add* about¦361038 the ten¦361041 horns on¦361043 its¦361043 head¦361043, and the other horn that came¦361046 up¦361046 later, with¦361043 three¦361053 \add other\add* horns falling¦361047. That horn had eyes¦361056 and a mouth¦361058 that said amazing things, and it¦361046 looked \add ≈stronger\add* than¦361063 the others.
\v 21 I¦361068 kept watching, and that¦361070 horn¦361069 was¦361071 waging war¦361072 with¦361073 those followers and \add ≈gaining over\add* them\x + \xo 7:21: \xt Rev 13:7.\x*
\v 22 until the ancient¦361083 \add one\add* arrived and¦361085 \add ≈judged¦361085 in favour¦361087 of his followers\add*. Then the time¦361089 arrived when \add *they¦361092\add* took¦361092 possession¦361092 of the kingdom¦361091.\x + \xo 7:22: \xt Rev 20:4.\x*
\p
\v 23 Then he¦361096 said¦361096:
\mi The fourth creature will be a fourth kingdom¦361099 in¦361103 the world which¦361104 will be different¦361105 from¦361106 all¦361108 the \add other\add* kingdoms¦361110. It¦361111 will devour¦361111 the whole¦361108 world, and trample¦361115 it down¦361115 and crush¦361116 it.
\v 24 As for the ten horns¦361118, ten kings will \add ≈become prominent\add* in that kingdom¦361121, and then¦361118 another¦361125 \add ≡king\add* will appear. He will be¦361129 different¦361129 from¦361130 the previous \add ≡kings\add*, and he¦361135 will \add ≈overcome\add* three¦361133 \add *of them\add*.\x + \xo 7:24: \xt Rev 17:12.\x*
\v 25 He will speak¦361141 \add ≈out\add* against¦361138 the highest one and will wear¦361144 out the highest one's \add ≈followers\add*. He will intend to change¦361146 the times and the law¦361148, and \add indeed\add* \add ≈he'll¦361141 be¦361149 allowed to\add* for a two times and a half.\x + \xo 7:25: \xt Rev 12:14; 13:5-6.\x*
\v 26 But¦361158 the¦361158 court¦361158 will sit¦361159, and then¦361158 his¦361160 \add ≈authority to rule\add* will be taken away—to \add ≈perish and be forever destroyed¦361163\add*.
\v 27 Then¦361168 the kingdom and the \add ≈authority to rule\add* and the greatness¦361170 of the kingdoms¦361172 under¦361173 all the heavens¦361176 will be given¦361177 to the people¦361178 \add ≈who are the followers\add* of the highest one. His kingdom will \add ≈continue forever¦361183\add*, and all \add ≈earthly rulers\add* will serve and obey¦361188 him.\x + \xo 7:27: a \xt Rev 20:4; \xo b \xt Rev 22:5.\x*
\p
\v 28 \add ≈That's everything that I saw\add* \add in my visions\add*. As for me, Daniel¦361198, my thoughts¦361201 \add ≈made me very upset\add* and¦361206 my face¦361203 turned pale, \add ≈but¦361203\add* I kept¦361208 the¦361206 matter¦361206 \add ≈to myself\add*.
\c 8
\s1 Daniel's sheep and goat vision
\rem /s1 Daniel's Vision of a Ram and Goat; Daniel's Vision of a Ram and a Goat; Vision of a Ram and a Goat
\p
\v 1 In¦361211 the third year¦361211 of¦361214 King¦361215 Belshatstsar's¦361214 reign¦361213, I¦361219, Daniel¦361220, had \add ≈another\add* vision¦361216—\add different from\add* the previous one.
\v 2 In¦361227 the vision¦361227, I saw¦361229 that¦361233 I was¦361231 at¦361227 the fortress in Shusan (\add often called Susa,\add* in Eylam province¦361235), beside the Ulai¦361243 Canal¦361242.
\v 3 Then¦361245 I¦361247 \add ≈looked up¦361245\add*, and \add ≈to my surprise, there was\add* a ram¦361250 standing¦361252 beside the canal¦361254. It¦361255 had two¦361256 horns and both¦361256 horns were¦361258 long, but¦361245 one was longer than¦361261 the other, and the longer one \add ≈had grown¦361265\add* last¦361266.
\v 4 I saw¦361268 the¦361271 ram charging westward¦361273, and northward¦361274, and southward¦361275—no animal could stand¦361281 \add ≈against¦361282 it\add*—there¦361283 was¦361283 no one¦361284 to¦361275 rescue \add ≈anything from¦361285 it\add*. \add *The ram\add* did¦361286 \add ≈whatever it wanted, and it became¦361288 very powerful¦361285\add*.
\p
\v 5 As I was observing¦361293, suddenly a billy goat was coming¦361298 from¦361299 the west¦361301, \add ≈crossing the entire¦361305 world\add* without¦361308 touching¦361309 the ground. The goat had a prominent horn¦361312 between¦361314 its¦361315 eyes¦361315.
\v 6 In a powerful rage¦361330, it charged at¦361330 the two-horned ram¦361320 that¦361323 I¦361324 had¦361324 seen¦361324 standing¦361325 in¦361330 front¦361326 of the canal¦361327.
\v 7 I saw¦361333 \add @the furious goat\add* \add ≈reach\add* the ram and strike¦361340 it, shattering \add @the ram's¦361343\add* two¦361347 horns¦361348. The ram \add ≈wasn't strong enough to stay standing¦361354 so\add* it was knocked to the ground and trampled¦361358 on¦361353—there was no one who could rescue the ram from¦361364 \add @the goat's\add* power¦361364.
\p
\v 8 Then¦361366 the billy goat \add ≈became¦361372 even more powerful¦361372, but¦361366 at its peak\add*, the large¦361375 horn¦361374 was¦361373 broken¦361373, and¦361366 in its place¦361379, four prominent horns came up \add ≈facing in the four different compass directions\add*.
\v 9 Then¦361384 a small¦361392 horn¦361389 grew out¦361388 of one¦361391 of them—it¦361388 grew very long towards the south¦361398, towards the east¦361401, and¦361384 towards the beautiful¦361404 land \add of Yisrael\add*.
\v 10 It¦361406 grew up to the army in the heavens¦361410, \add ≈but¦361406\add* it threw¦361411 some of the army and some of the stars¦361418 down to the earth and trampled¦361419 on them.\x + \xo 8:10: \xt Rev 12:4.\x*
\v 11 It \add ≈became as powerful\add* as the \add ≈general\add* \add ≈leading\add* the \add heavenly\add* army. It took the regular¦361429 burnt¦361429 offering¦361429 away from him¦361426, and¦361421 his sanctuary¦361432 place¦361431 was overthrown¦361430.
\v 12 Because of \add ≈sinfulness\add*, \add ≈it will be allowed to take control of\add* the army¦361434, along with¦361439 the regular¦361438 burnt¦361438 offering¦361438. It¦361440 will \add ≈overthrow\add* truth¦361441, \add ≈but¦361434 it will be active\add* and¦361434 will prosper¦361444.
\p
\v 13 Then¦361446 I heard¦361446 a holy one\f + \fr 8:13 \ft Presumably these ‘holy ones’ are angels in the heavens (not ‘saints’ like in 7:27)???\f* speaking, and another holy one said¦361451 to that particular one who¦361455 was¦361455 speaking, “How long until¦361456 this vision¦361459 about the regular¦361460 burnt¦361460 offering¦361460 \add will be fulfilled\add*, \add ≡and about\add* the transgression¦361461 that makes desolate¦361462, the giving¦361463 over of the sanctuary¦361464 and the army¦361465 to be trampled¦361466 on?”
\p
\v 14 “For 2,300 evenings¦361471 and¦361468 mornings¦361472,” he¦361468 said¦361468 to¦361469 me¦361469, “Then¦361468 the¦361477 sanctuary¦361477 will¦361476 be¦361476 restored¦361476.”
\s1 Gavri'el explains Daniel's vision
\rem /s1 Gabriel Interprets the Vision; Gabriel Explains the Vision; The Interpretation of the Vision; The Angel Gabriel Explains the Vision
\p
\v 15 While I, Daniel¦361482, \add ≈was still trying to make sense of the vision¦361485 that I'd seen¦361480, suddenly someone who looked like a man¦361493 was standing¦361489 in¦361480 front¦361490 of\add* me.
\v 16 Then¦361495 I heard¦361495 a human voice¦361496 call¦361501 out from the Ulai¦361500 \add canal\add*, “Gavri'el¦361503 \add !(Gabriel¦361503)\add*, \add ≈help that¦361505\add* man \add to be able to\add* understand the vision¦361508.”\x + \xo 8:16: \xt Luk 1:19,26.\x*
\v 17 So¦361510 he came \add over\add* near where¦361512 I stood, \add ≈but¦361510\add* I was frightened and¦361510 fell¦361515 \add ≈to my knees with¦361513 my face¦361518 to the ground\add*. \add ≈However,\add* he said¦361519 to me¦361520, “You, human¦361524 male, that¦361525 vision¦361529 \add ≈concerns\add* the end¦361528 \add of the world\add*.”
\p
\v 18 While he was speaking¦361531 with me, I¦361533 fell into a \add ≈trance\add* with my¦361536 face¦361536 \add still\add* on¦361531 the¦361537 ground¦361537. Then¦361531 he touched¦361538 me and¦361531 made¦361541 me stand¦361541 \add ≈up\add*
\v 19 and¦361546 he¦361546 said¦361546, “\add ≈Listen\add*, I'm¦361547 going to \add ≈tell\add* you¦361548 what will¦361552 happen \add ≈towards the end\add* of \add God's\add* indignation¦361554, because¦361555 it¦361556 refers¦361556 to the appointed¦361556 time¦361556 of the end.
\p
\v 20 The¦361563 ram¦361559 that¦361560 you¦361562 saw¦361562 with the two¦361564 horns¦361564 represents the kings¦361565 of¦361565 Media¦361566 and¦361567 Persia¦361567,
\v 21 and the shaggy¦361570 goat¦361569 is the Greece's¦361572 king, and the large¦361574 horn¦361573 between¦361576 his eyes¦361578 is \add &their\add* first¦361581 king.
